A brief overview of key terms:

`<package>`
	A package file or package name, depending on the command.

`<package@version>`
	Full package name consisting of package name and version.

`--aarch64`
	Select the aarch64 processor architecture.

`--x86_64`
	Select the x86_64 processor architecture.

(1) INFO ABOUT UTILITY
=======================================================================================================

`catpkg` is the package manager for the CATPKG package system.

It is used to install, remove, verify, update and inspect installed packages.
It also provides a package build command.

The package format used by catpkg is `.catpackage`.

The package metadata is stored according to the CATPKG metadata specification.

Permissions:

`install`
Root privileges required.

`remove`
Root privileges required.

`update`
Root privileges required.

`verify`
Default user permissions.

`database`
Default user permissions.

`build`
Default user permissions.

# (2) COMMANDS
=======================================================================================================

## (2.1) INSTALL
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`catpkg install <package>`

Installs a `.catpackage` file.

Example:

`sudo catpkg install ./catpkg-x86_64@1.0.0.catpackage`

Before installation, catpkg displays information about the package
and asks for confirmation.

The command requires root privileges.

## (2.2) UPDATE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`catpkg update <package>`

Updates an already installed package using the specified `.catpackage` file.

Example:

`sudo catpkg update ./catpkg-x86_64@1.0.1.catpackage`

The package name and version are read from `PACKAGEINFO`.

The command requires root privileges.

## (2.3) REMOVE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`catpkg remove <package>`

Removes an installed package.

Example:

`sudo catpkg remove catpkg`

If only one installed version matches the package name,
that version is selected automatically.

If several versions are installed, catpkg asks the user
which version should be removed.

The command requires root privileges.

## (2.4) VERIFY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`catpkg verify <package>`

Verifies the integrity of an installed package.

Example:

`catpkg verify catpkg`

If the package name matches several packages, catpkg displays
the available matches.

If several installed versions exist, catpkg asks for the full
package name:

`name@version`

The verification process uses the package metadata and package integrity information.

## (2.5) DATABASE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`catpkg database list`

Displays information about installed packages.

Example:

`catpkg database list`

The package list contains:

`Name`
Package name.

`Version`
Installed package versions.

`Description`
Package description from `PACKAGEINFO`.

`Dependencies`
Package dependencies declared in `PACKAGEINFO`.

## (2.6) BUILD
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`catpkg build`

Builds a package using the default architecture.

Architecture can be selected explicitly:

`catpkg build --x86_64`

`catpkg build --aarch64`

The available architectures are:

`x86_64`
64-bit x86 architecture.

`aarch64`
64-bit ARM architecture.

When no architecture option is specified, the build process uses its default configuration.

## (2.7) HELP
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`catpkg help`

Displays the catpkg command help.

Example:

`catpkg help`

# (3) PACKAGE NAME
=======================================================================================================

A full package name is written in the following form:

`name@version`

Example:

`catpkg@1.0.0-beta`

The package name and version are taken from the `PACKAGEINFO`
metadata stored inside the `.catpackage` file.

# (4) PACKAGE FILE
=======================================================================================================

A package installed by catpkg must use the `.catpackage` extension.

Example:

`catpkg-x86_64@1.0.0.catpackage`

Before installation or updating, catpkg checks the package format
and reads its `PACKAGEINFO`.

The metadata contains information such as:

* package name;
* package version;
* architecture;
* description;
* SHA;
* package size;
* dependencies;
* package operations.

The complete metadata format is documented separately in:

`CATPKG_METADATA_SPECIFICATION`

# (5) ARCHITECTURE OPTIONS
=======================================================================================================

Architecture options currently supported by the command line interface:

`--x86_64`

```
Build for x86_64.
```

`--aarch64`

```
Build for aarch64.
```

These options are currently handled by:

`catpkg build`

Examples:

`catpkg build --x86_64`

`catpkg build --aarch64`

Architecture selection does not change the command itself;
it changes the target architecture passed to the build system.

# (6) ROOT PRIVILEGES

The following commands require administrator privileges:

`catpkg install`
`catpkg update`
`catpkg remove`

When these commands are executed without root privileges,
catpkg prints an error and recommends running the command
with `sudo` or as root.

Example:

`sudo catpkg install ./package.catpackage`

